{
  "coreName": "Flycast",
  "systemNames": {
    "DC": "Dreamcast",
    "NAOMI": "Sega NAOMI",
    "NAOMI2": "Sega NAOMI 2",
    "AW": "Sammy Atomiswave"
  },
  "author": "Flyinghead and the Flycast contributors",
  "url": "https://github.com/flyinghead/flycast",
  "romFile": "disc",
  "deterministic": true,
  "memoryLayoutMiB": [
    256,
    16,
    64,
    16,
    1536
  ],
  "_memoryLayoutMiB_note": "sbrk, sealed, invisible, plain, mmap. A Dreamcast is BIG: 16MB of system RAM, 16MB of video RAM, 8MB for the AICA and 32MB for the Elan, and with no virtual memory in a sandbox those are ordinary allocations inside the guest heap. Sized like a smaller machine, an allocation fails quietly and the machine writes through a null base. The MMAP arena is the one that had to grow for the OpenGL renderer, and not the heap: musl sends every LARGE allocation to mmap, and a whole Mesa is mostly large allocations. With it at 256 MiB OSMesaCreateContext simply returned null, and growing sbrk instead changed nothing.",
  "video": {
    "greenzoneSurvivesReload": true,
    "_comment_greenzone": "TRUE: a project's greenzone this core drew on a GPU may be loaded by a later session (docs/gpu-bridge.md in Chimera, 'Keeping the greenzone across sessions'). Measured 2026-09-16: Re-Volt, 1000 frames, the greenzone (21.7 MB) written at frame 900 and loaded in a fresh process; frame 999 identical (0 of 307,200 pixels differ, 900 colours), a control frame (850, in the other phase of the picture's 400-frame cycle) differing by 98.52%. Until 2026-10-18 this was flycast's name on a list in Chimera.",
    "gpuStatesSurviveTheContext": true,
    "drawEveryFrame": true,
    "_comment_drawEveryFrame": "Never tell this core to stop drawing; turbo skips only the readback. The turbo patch (0010) returns from OpenGLRenderer::Render before the pass that reaches a screen, and what this renderer draws lives on the far side of the GPU bridge and PERSISTS there - so a screen a game paints once and then leaves alone is painted by exactly one frame, and skipping that frame loses it. No warm-up recovers it: measured on Re-Volt, a seek to frame 1500 shows the SEGA licence screen instead of the title, and drawing the last 1, 4, 6, 8, 12, 30, 60 or 120 frames changes nothing - only 300, which reaches back past the paint. Drawing throughout is nearly free: 1500 frames on a GTX 1060 cost 14.8s in turbo, 15.0s drawing, 17.5s drawing AND reading back.",
    "_comment_gpuStates": "The renderer rebuilds its GL objects when the calls land on a context that is not the one they came from (the bridge's GL_OP_CONTEXT_ID), so a state this core makes opens again in a later session - its greenzone survives a reload instead of being discarded.",
    "_comment": "buffer CAPACITY; the live size comes from GetVideoWidth/Height per frame, which follow the tile clip and the scaler registers a game sets. 640x480 is the most a Dreamcast displays - and 480x640 is what a vertical arcade game becomes once the picture is turned the way its cabinet's monitor was mounted (the rotation setting), which is why the two are equal. Both are three times that because internalResolution lets an OpenGL renderer draw the same frame at up to 3x: 1920x1440, or 1440x1920 turned. This is what the HOST reserves; what a project costs in its own states is what it actually draws, so a project at 1x costs what it always did.",
    "width": 1920,
    "height": 1920,
    "virtualWidth": 640,
    "virtualHeight": 480,
    "vsyncNumerator": 60000,
    "vsyncDenominator": 1001,
    "getBgra": "GetVideoBgra"
  },
  "audio": {
    "samplesPerFrame": 8192,
    "channels": 2,
    "get": "GetAudio"
  },
  "lag": {
    "inputWasRead": "InputWasRead"
  },
  "_machines_note": "ONE core.wbx, four machines. Flycast is a Dreamcast, and the arcade boards Sega built out of the same parts: the NAOMI (a Dreamcast with more RAM, a cartridge or GD-ROM board and a JVS I/O board in place of the controller ports), the NAOMI 2 (two PowerVRs and the Elan geometry chip in front of them) and Sammy's Atomiswave (a NAOMI with a different cartridge slot and its own bios). What they share stays above; what makes them different machines is below. Which one a session is comes from the machine setting, so a project pins it and a movie cites it. The three arcade machines have the same panel because they have the same I/O board. A rom set decides which board it is for - Flycast's own romset table says - and a set for another board is refused at load, the way a Game Gear cartridge is refused by a Mega Drive: the machine is the project's, not the file's. The System SP (the card-reader boards) is left out: it needs its own bios and readers this core does not drive.",
  "machineSetting": "machine",
  "machines": [
    {
      "id": "DC",
      "label": "Dreamcast",
      "when": [
        "dreamcast"
      ],
      "input": {
        "name": "Dreamcast Controller",
        "_comment": "FOUR PORTS, and one superset of controls covering every device any of them may be set to (the port<N> settings). A Dreamcast port takes more than a gamepad, and the declared control list is static - it cannot follow the settings - so every device's controls are declared for every player and a port ignores the ones its device does not have. The controller family (gamepad, arcadeStick, twinStick, xl) all read the SAME state and differ only in which parts of it the device reports: the retail pad has no C, D or Z and no second d-pad, the twin stick has the second d-pad and no analog at all, and the PantherDC 'xl' has everything including a second analog stick. Mouse and lightGun are different wires: the mouse's axes are RELATIVE motion and the gun's are an absolute position on the screen, which is why they are separate axes rather than the stick's.",
